// Flags uses of the `Date` *value* (constructor, statics, prototype) while
// leaving the `Date` *type* alone. Biome's `noRestrictedGlobals` rule cannot
// make that distinction — it matches every reference to the identifier,
// including type annotations — so this is a syntactic plugin instead.
//
// The point is code that can end up in the MAIN-world content script, where the
// global `Date` is FakeDate: it must go through `date/original-date` to get
// the native implementation. The `$filename` guard below therefore limits the
// rule to src/content-scripts/ and src/util/ — the popup trees (components/,
// display/, popup/, tab-state/), the tests and the build config run with a real
// `Date` and are not restricted. `overrides` in biome.json cannot switch plugins
// off (its `plugins` field only adds to the top-level list), so the path filter
// has to live here. Note that groups in the regex must be non-capturing — Grit
// reads a plain `(...)` as a capture variable and errors out.
//
// Known gap: aliasing the global (`const D = Date; new D()`) is not detected —
// which is what makes `export const OriginalDate = Date` in original-date.ts
// pass. Suppress with `// biome-ignore lint/plugin: <reason>` (plain `plugin:`
// does not work).

language js

or {
    `new Date($args)`,
    `Date.$prop`,
    `$x instanceof Date`,
    `class $c extends Date { $body }`
} as $usage where {
    $filename <: r".*/src/(?:content-scripts|util)/.*",
    register_diagnostic(span=$usage, message="Do not use the `Date` constructor or its statics, always use OriginalDate or FakeDate explicitly.", severity="error")
}
